# A name bound to what the language's own code computes of names, with no call, stands for those names (#328): `named`
# binds the gap between the two levels (`first.db - second.db`) and the array of both (`const both: Level[] = [first,
# second]`, whose type is written) to names before it hands each to a step, and `written` writes each where its step is
# handed it. An operator is no occurrence and a name adds no arrow, so the two routes are one plan: each step takes the
# two levels, and no value of the body's own (`gap`, `both`) stands between them and the step.

route signal.named
step named/draw
step named/show

line entry > named call
line Level·first > named value
line Level·second > named value
line Level·first > named/draw value                  # `draw(gap)`, `const gap = first.db - second.db`
line Level·second > named/draw value
line Level·first > named/show value                  # `show(both)`, `const both: Level[] = [first, second]`
line Level·second > named/show value
line named/draw > Screen own
line named/show > Screen own

home Level·first named
home Level·second named

side entry left
side Screen right

route signal.written
step written/draw
step written/show

line entry > written call
line Level·first > written value
line Level·second > written value
line Level·first > written/draw value                # `draw(first.db - second.db)`
line Level·second > written/draw value
line Level·first > written/show value                # `show([first, second])`
line Level·second > written/show value
line written/draw > Screen own
line written/show > Screen own

home Level·first written
home Level·second written

side entry left
side Screen right
